  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Every Demeanor option is exposed as a <Demeanor*> MSBuild property, and every protection takes a level rather than an on/off switch: <DemeanorRename>off|on|aggressive</DemeanorRename>, <DemeanorCfg>none|reorder|predicates|flatten</DemeanorCfg>, and off|on for string, constant and resource encryption, call hiding, anti-tamper, anti-debug, enum deletion and each kind of symbol renaming. Each is empty by default, meaning the strongest level. Symbol and assembly lists are items, and a pattern or a regular expression goes in Pattern metadata: <DemeanorExcludeSymbol Include=\"dto-types\" Pattern=\"MyApp.Dto.*\" />. MSBuild reads an item's Include as a file specification, so a pattern belongs in metadata, where it reaches the obfuscator exactly as written; DEM0001 reports a regular expression placed in Include. Project rules and resolved decisions live in a .demeanor directory beside the project and travel with the repository; <DemeanorFailOnPendingDecisions> turns an unresolved decision into a build failure. Compiled XAML (BAML) tracks renamed types, including plain CLR classes used from markup. An expired Enterprise licence fails the build rather than producing a partially protected assembly. The obfuscator ships inside the package for Windows x64 and Linux x64. Pricing and purchase: https://www.wiseowlsoftware.com/pricing.",
